Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// The previous strategy was syntactically safe but semantically weak for Spider:
// it let the model wander token-by-token inside SQL, which often produced short
// locally-valid but globally-bad fragments like "SELECT <id> <id> ,". The key
// change here is to make constrained decoding much more deterministic and much
// more eager to terminate once a complete SQL query is available.
//
// Revised strategy:
// 1. Outside the constrained span, emit exactly one token per outer step with
//    UnconstrainedStep until we hit "<<" or EOS. This keeps cost accounting
//    simple and preserves the required << >> framing.
// 2. Inside the constrained span, if the SQL prefix is already complete, close
//    immediately. This strongly biases toward a single finished query instead of
//    trailing junk.
// 3. Otherwise, ask the parser how many valid next tokens exist. When the set is
//    small, use TopValidCandidates and deterministically choose among parser-
//    valid options by heavily boosting them and penalizing EOS. This reduces the
//    chance of malformed local continuations.
// 4. When the valid set is larger, still use parser-aware ConstrainedStep, but
//    only after constructing the stable prompt outside the active SQL suffix.
// 5. If the parser reports a dead end, roll back the constrained segment to the
//    longest valid prefix and continue from there instead of drifting deeper into
//    an unrecoverable fragment.
// 6. EOS is treated as terminal everywhere.
//
// This directly targets the observed failures: fewer free choices inside SQL,
// immediate closure on complete parses, and rollback from dead ends instead of
// accumulating garbage tokens.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity:
// - Unconstrained outside-span branch: UnconstrainedStep may append a token to
//   generated, but insideConstrainedOut stays false unless the token opens "<<"
//   via OpenConstrainedSpan; that helper sets currentConstrainedOut := [], and
//   parser.IsValidPrefix([]) holds.
// - Complete-prefix close branch: CloseConstrainedSpan exits constrained mode,
//   so the implication is vacuous afterward.
// - Dead-end rollback branch: RollbackConstrainedSpan returns a rolled-back
//   constrained prefix that is valid by construction, so validity is preserved.
// - Small-valid-set and regular constrained branches: if next == eosToken we
//   break without changing constrained state; otherwise AppendConstrainedToken is
//   used only with parser-valid next tokens (from TopValidCandidates or
//   ConstrainedStep), so the resulting prefix remains valid.
//
// suffix:
// - Outside-span unconstrained branch: when we stay outside, the implication is
//   vacuous; when we open a span, currentConstrainedOut becomes [], whose suffix
//   relation holds trivially.
// - Close branch: after CloseConstrainedSpan we are outside constrained mode, so
//   the implication is vacuous.
// - Rollback branch: RollbackConstrainedSpan rewrites generated and
//   currentConstrainedOut together so that the active constrained suffix equals
//   the returned constrained prefix.
// - Append branches: AppendConstrainedToken appends the same token to generated
//   and currentConstrainedOut, preserving suffix equality.
//
// cost accounting:
// - Every outer iteration either uses one cost-bumping helper
//   (UnconstrainedStep, OpenConstrainedSpan, CloseConstrainedSpan,
//   ConstrainedStep) or one explicit ChooseNextToken call with a manual helper
//   cost bump, and then increments steps by exactly 1.
// - The rollback/query helpers do not bump cost. Therefore steps counts outer
//   progress and remains within maxSteps.
//
// progress:
// - Each outer iteration increments steps by exactly 1.
// - UnconstrainedStep appends at most one token; if that token is "<<", an
//   additional OpenConstrainedSpan may append delimiter bookkeeping text but the
//   total per outer step is still bounded by stepTokenBudget as required by the
//   strategy design.
// - CloseConstrainedSpan appends at most the closing delimiter for one step.
// - Rollback can only shorten generated, never increase it.
// - AppendConstrainedToken adds one token, and the EOS branches append nothing.
//   Hence generated grows by at most the allowed per-step budget, preserving
//   |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps * stepTokenBudget.
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_END
generated := generatedPrefix;
insideConstrainedOut := insideConstrained;
currentConstrainedOut := currentConstrained;
cost := 0;

var steps := 0;

while steps < maxSteps
  invariant 0 <= steps <= maxSteps
  invariant lm.ValidTokensIdsLogits()
  invariant !insideConstrainedOut ==> currentConstrainedOut == []
  invariant insideConstrainedOut ==> parser.IsValidPrefix(currentConstrainedOut)
  invariant insideConstrainedOut ==> |currentConstrainedOut| <= |generated|
  invariant insideConstrainedOut ==> generated[|generated| - |currentConstrainedOut|..] == currentConstrainedOut
  invariant |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps * stepTokenBudget
  invariant cost == 0
  decreases maxSteps - steps
